Fall back to WebGL2 when a browser's WebGPU canvases show nothing.

What: gpui_web's automatic backend choice (`WebBackendPreference::Auto`) gets one more WebGPU check after the device is
made, in a new file `webgpu_canvas_probe.rs` behind a nine-line hook in platform.rs: `shows_drawing` clears a detached
4x4 WebGPU canvas to red and reads it back through a 2D `OffscreenCanvas` in the same task. Anything but opaque red
runs `fall_back_to_webgl2`: it logs `WebGPU initialization failed; falling back to WebGL2: WebGPU draws nothing to a
canvas here` and tries WebGL2 on a new canvas while keeping the WebGPU device, once more after the next
requestAnimationFrame if the first try fails (a context refused while the page starts up). WebGL2 working, the WebGPU canvas is
removed and the window draws with WebGL2. WebGL2 failing twice (its `webglcontextcreationerror` status messages are in
the error), the window stays on the WebGPU device with an error logged (`WebGL2 initialization failed too (..);
staying on WebGPU, whose canvas test read back blank: this window may show nothing`): a window that may show nothing
beats no window. An app that asks for WebGPU explicitly is not probed.

Why: Chromium 158 (Fedora's build) on NVIDIA's proprietary Linux driver (RTX 5060, Wayland) gives a working NVIDIA
WebGPU adapter whose canvases composite black whenever Chromium's Vulkan feature is on (`--enable-features=Vulkan`,
with `--use-angle=vulkan`, headed or headless), and its default flags give a SwiftShader adapter that does the same:
a ten-line page clearing a WebGPU canvas red shows black, and drawImage of it reads 0,0,0,0. GPUI then showed an
all-black window (seen on the owner's desktop, 2026-11-28). The same read gives 255,0,0,255 where WebGPU works
(`--use-angle=gl`). The choice is private to gpui_web's platform (no extension point).

Tests: in a browser (docs/web-webgpu.md): with `--enable-features=Vulkan --use-angle=vulkan` hello and examples/native-view
log the fallback and draw with WebGL2; with `--use-angle=gl` they keep WebGPU; with `--disable-webgl` as well as a
blank WebGPU they log both failures and still open the window.

Upstream PR: not filed (filing paused, patch rule 14).

